\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A unit mass need not make ring precision invertible}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Over #{\mathbb Z}, choose the vertex order #{(0,1)} and the basis
map #{f\mapsto(f(0),f(1))} from the finite function module to
#{\mathbb Z^2}. A unit-weight edge with #{\mu=1} has
#{Q=\begin{pmatrix}2&-1\\-1&2\end{pmatrix}}.
Solving #{Q(x,y)=(1,0)} would require #{x=2y} and #{3y=1}, which
is impossible in #{\mathbb Z}. This #{Q} is not even surjective.}

\p{This is the counting-basis specialization of the incidence and
precision construction in \ref{fdip-003D}. The energy convention
comes from \citet{Section 2.1, pp. 4-5}{spielman2009effective};
the failure of integral surjectivity is the displayed calculation.}
